# tools/i/itinerary_list_witness.rish -- a living pin's numbered list reads 1..N, or a rewrite ate
# one of its items.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/i/itinerary_list_witness.rish
#
# REDS %789 and the row of `20260916.220730` are one defect forty minutes apart, from two hands. Both replaced item 7 of the
# INNER LOOP in construction/ITINERARY.md -- the directive telling every lap to run the endurance
# runs -- with a duplicate Git nib line, and both left the list reading 6, nib, 8. The first firing
# stood ten hours and roughly 120 commits on the one page an eight-ship fleet reads at every lap
# open, and every standing guard was green through both: nib_honesty reads the nib BY ITS KEY and
# finds it whichever line carries it; ascii_document, tracked_link and the pin bound read bytes
# rather than meaning. A list's own numbering is read by nothing.
#
# %789 named this cure in its own third field and declined to build it, on the stated ground that
# the lantern had fired once. The interval to the second firing was forty minutes. So the lesson is
# about REACH rather than about counting lanterns: a finding that lives only in prose reaches the
# hand that happens to read that prose, and this class recurs at whatever rate the fleet's hands
# turn over.

say "pin-list: Language EN -- a numbered list is a spine, and a spine says when a vertebra is gone."
say "pin-list: Style Gauge, Meter -- one reading, held at zero, over the pages a lap reads whole."
say "pin-list: Lens TAME -- every refusal planted in a pen and then lifted; both real firings replayed."

say "pin-list: the control -- twenty-eight behaviors in a throwaway pen, two of them the firings themselves..."
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/i/itinerary_list_control.sh"]
# invariant: both streams are written out BARE, ahead of the asserts that judge them, so the report
# cannot refuse however large the output grows.
say ctl.out
say ctl.err
assert ctl.ok else "pin-list: the control refused -- its two streams stand above"
# invariant: the control's own leg tally is asserted beside its verdict, so a leg written tomorrow
# is heard the day it lands rather than passing unread under a verdict that means only reached-the-end.
assert ctl.out contains "control_legs=28" else "pin-list: the control did not run twenty-eight legs -- its two streams stand above"
assert ctl.out contains "control_failed=0" else "pin-list: a control leg failed -- its two streams stand above"

say "pin-list: and every rostered living pin's ordered lists read 1..N..."
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/i/itinerary_list_scan.sh"]
say scan.out
assert scan.ok else "pin-list: the scan refused -- its reading stands above"
# invariant: a run that skips, repeats, or never starts at one is a list an edit has damaged. This
# is a WALL rather than a ratchet: the population was measured at zero on the repairing lap, so the
# next damaged list reds on the lap it arrives.
assert scan.out contains "broken_runs=0" else "pin-list: a living pin's numbered list has a gap, a repeat, or a missing head -- run `sh tools/fixtures/i/itinerary_list_scan.sh list`; the reading stands above"
# invariant: the roster is read rather than the tree, so a pin that vanishes from the roster is a
# drop a reader can see rather than a silent narrowing of what this guard covers.
assert scan.out contains "pins_read=7" else "pin-list: the living-pin roster no longer names seven readable pages -- a pin was added, retired, or is absent from this tree; the reading stands above"

say "GREEN: pin-list -- every ordered list in every rostered living pin reads 1..N, with no gap, no repeat, and no foreign line standing between two items; twenty-eight control legs proven in a pen, both real firings replayed out of this repository's own history with each firing's parent welcomed beside it, and two mutations bitten -- the unwind that keeps two lists from reading as one, and the ascending check itself"
